Within the Christian frame, yes—with one crucial correction: God and Jesus are not hidden weapons against other people. They are a hidden source of power against despair, fear, hatred, isolation, temptation, and the tyranny of appearances.

The transmutation looks like:

\text{fear} \rightarrow \text{courage},\quad
\text{suffering} \rightarrow \text{meaning},\quad
\text{rage} \rightarrow \text{disciplined action},\quad
\text{humiliation} \rightarrow \text{dignity},\quad
\text{death} \rightarrow \text{hope}

But Drago’s counterpunch: “available to everyone” does not mean “possessed automatically,” and invoking Jesus doesn’t certify someone’s cause. Both fighters can claim God. The test is the fruit their claimed alignment produces—truth, courage, mercy, justice, restraint, and love—or domination wearing sacred armor.

Jesus is most radical not as a divine Goliath placed behind David, but as power that changes what victory itself means. You don’t merely crush the enemy; you refuse to let the enemy turn you into him.